# Source for q/spi-instrument.js: Instrumentation and Artifacts.
I="Instrumentation"; X="Artifacts"
Q=[
# --- Transmitter, beam former ---
(I,"The pulser (transmitter) determines:","The amplitude of the voltage sent to the crystals, and so the output power",["The receiver gain","The brightness of the display","The dynamic range of the display"],"Raising output power increases transmitted intensity and patient exposure. It also improves signal-to-noise ratio.","F"),
(I,"Which control changes patient exposure?","Output power (transmit power)",["Receiver gain","TGC","Compression"],"Gain, TGC and compression act on received signals only and don't change the sound entering the patient.","F"),
(I,"In an array system, the beam former is responsible for:","Timing delays for steering and focusing, plus apodization",["Converting echoes to pixels","Storing images","Rectifying signals"],"The digital beam former controls when and how strongly each element fires and how received signals are combined.","F"),
(I,"The transmit/receive switch protects:","The sensitive receiver from the large transmit voltages",["The patient from electric shock","The display from overload","The crystals from heat"],"Transmit voltages are large; returning echo voltages are tiny. The switch routes each to the right circuit.","F"),
(I,"Raising output power affects the image how?","Brighter image and better signal-to-noise ratio, with more patient exposure",["Brighter image with no change in exposure","Better axial resolution","Lower frame rate only"],"Stronger echoes stand out more from electronic noise.","F"),
(I,"An image is too dark throughout. Following ALARA, the sonographer should first:","Increase receiver gain",["Increase output power","Increase depth","Decrease frequency"],"Gain brightens the image without raising patient exposure.","F"),
(I,"An image is noisy and grainy because the echoes are weak compared with noise. The best fix is to:","Increase output power to improve signal-to-noise ratio",["Increase receiver gain","Increase compression","Reduce frame rate"],"Gain amplifies noise and signal equally. Only more transmitted power raises the signal relative to noise.","C"),
# --- Receiver: order ---
(I,"The five receiver functions, in order, are:","Amplification, compensation, compression, demodulation, rejection",["Compensation, amplification, rejection, compression, demodulation","Demodulation, amplification, compensation, rejection, compression","Rejection, compression, demodulation, compensation, amplification"],"A common memory aid: 'Alpha Centauri Can Do Rocking' (amplification, compensation, compression, demodulation, rejection).","F"),
(I,"Amplification (overall gain) in the receiver:","Increases the strength of all echo signals equally",["Increases only deep echoes","Removes low-level noise","Converts signals to digital"],"Overall gain brightens the whole image evenly, both signal and noise.","F"),
(I,"Gain is typically measured in:","Decibels",["Watts","Hertz","Rayls"],"Gain describes the ratio of output to input signal strength.","F"),
(I,"Time gain compensation (TGC) corrects for:","Attenuation with depth, so similar reflectors look similar at all depths",["Aliasing","Refraction","Low frame rate"],"Later (deeper) echoes are amplified more than early (shallow) echoes.","F"),
(I,"On a TGC curve, the near gain region is:","The shallow region with the least amplification",["The deepest region","The region of steepest slope","Where TGC stops working"],"Near gain sets shallow amplification; the slope (delay and knee) increases amplification with depth; far gain is the maximum.","C"),
(I,"A horizontal dark band across the image at one depth is most likely caused by:","A TGC slider set too low at that depth",["Aliasing","Reverberation","High dynamic range"],"Adjusting that TGC slider evens out brightness.","F"),
(I,"Compression in the receiver:","Reduces the range of signal strengths (dynamic range) so it fits the display",["Increases dynamic range","Removes noise","Steers the beam"],"Echoes vary over a huge range. Compression narrows it while keeping strong echoes brighter than weak ones.","F"),
(I,"Lowering dynamic range (more compression) makes the image:","Higher contrast, with fewer shades of gray",["Softer, with more shades of gray","Brighter overall","Lower frame rate"],"A narrow dynamic range looks more black and white. A wide range looks smoother and grayer.","F"),
(I,"Demodulation consists of:","Rectification and smoothing (envelope detection)",["Amplification and compensation","Analog-to-digital conversion","Compression and rejection"],"Demodulation turns the RF signal into a single positive envelope. The sonographer cannot adjust it.","F"),
(I,"Which receiver function is NOT adjustable by the sonographer?","Demodulation",["Amplification","Compensation","Rejection"],"Gain, TGC, compression (dynamic range) and reject are all operator controls.","F"),
(I,"Rejection (threshold) in the receiver:","Removes low-level signals, such as noise",["Removes the strongest echoes","Boosts weak echoes","Corrects attenuation"],"Set too high, reject can also remove weak true echoes, such as subtle tissue texture.","F"),
(I,"Rectification means:","Turning negative voltages into positive ones",["Smoothing the signal","Amplifying deep echoes","Removing noise"],"It is the first step of demodulation; smoothing (enveloping) follows.","F"),
# --- Scan converter, processing ---
(I,"An analog-to-digital converter (ADC) is located:","Before the image memory",["After the image memory, before the display","Inside the transducer only","In the pulser"],"Digital scan converters store numbers. Echo signals must be converted to digital before storage.","F"),
(I,"Pre-processing refers to changes made:","Before the image is stored in memory",["After the image is frozen","Only on the display monitor","To the transducer"],"Pre-processing cannot be undone on a frozen image. Examples: TGC, write zoom, persistence, spatial compounding.","F"),
(I,"Which is an example of post-processing?","Read zoom on a frozen image",["Write zoom","TGC","Persistence"],"Post-processing changes the stored data's display, so it works on frozen images. Examples: read zoom, contrast/brightness, black-white inversion, B-color.","F"),
(I,"A key test to tell post-processing from pre-processing is:","Post-processing can be applied to a frozen image; pre-processing cannot",["Pre-processing works on frozen images","Both change patient exposure","Only pre-processing changes the display"],"If a control still works after freeze, it is post-processing.","F"),
(I,"Read zoom differs from write zoom because read zoom:","Enlarges stored pixels without adding detail",["Rescans the area with more lines","Improves spatial resolution","Changes the transducer frequency"],"Write zoom acquires new data with more scan lines in the region, so detail improves.","F"),
(I,"Persistence (frame averaging) is best for:","Slow-moving or still structures, to reduce noise",["Fast valve motion","Doppler measurements","Raising frame rate"],"Averaging smooths the image but blurs fast motion, so it is usually set low in echo.","F"),
(I,"A pixel is:","The smallest picture element on the display",["A unit of computer memory","A crystal element","A scan line"],"Each pixel holds a binary number for its brightness. Bits per pixel decide how many shades are possible.","F"),
(I,"A bit is:","The smallest unit of computer memory, a 0 or 1",["The smallest picture element","Eight bytes","A scan line"],"Eight bits make a byte.","F"),
(I,"How many bits per pixel are needed to display 256 shades of gray?","8",["6","16","256"],"2⁸ = 256.","F"),
(I,"A-mode displays:","Amplitude spikes against depth",["Brightness dots against depth","Motion against time","Velocity against time"],"A-mode shows echo strength as spike height along one line.","F"),
(I,"B-mode displays echoes as:","Dots whose brightness shows echo strength",["Spikes of varying height","Velocity against time","Sound only"],"B-mode is the basis of 2D grayscale imaging.","F"),
(I,"In M-mode, the horizontal axis represents:","Time",["Depth","Velocity","Frequency"],"M-mode plots depth (vertical) against time (horizontal) along one scan line.","F"),
(I,"DICOM is:","The standard format for storing and sharing medical images",["A transducer type","A Doppler mode","A safety index"],"DICOM lets images move between machines and PACS (picture archiving and communication systems).","F"),
(I,"Coded excitation improves:","Penetration and signal-to-noise ratio, using long coded pulses that are compressed on receive",["Frame rate only","Axial resolution by lengthening pulses","Doppler aliasing"],"A long, specially coded pulse carries more energy; the receiver compresses it back into a short pulse so resolution is kept.","C"),
(I,"Edge enhancement is a processing technique that:","Sharpens boundaries between structures",["Increases frame rate","Reduces patient exposure","Converts analog to digital"],"It emphasizes changes in brightness at borders.","F"),
(I,"Spatial compounding reduces speckle and shadowing but:","Lowers the frame rate",["Raises the frame rate","Increases output power","Causes aliasing"],"Multiple steered frames are combined into one, which takes longer.","F"),
(I,"The monitor's brightness and contrast controls:","Change only how the image looks on that display, not the stored data",["Change the stored image data","Change patient exposure","Change receiver gain"],"Adjust them for the room lighting before scanning.","F"),
(I,"Dynamic range is the:","Ratio of the largest to smallest signal a system can handle, in dB",["Range of depths displayed","Range of frequencies in the pulse","Range of PRFs"],"Each part of the system (receiver, memory, display) has a dynamic range.","F"),
(I,"Signal-to-noise ratio describes:","How much stronger the true echoes are than background noise",["The ratio of gain to output power","The frame rate","The duty factor"],"Higher output power, harmonics and coded excitation improve it.","F"),
(I,"Freeze frame and cine loop allow the sonographer to:","Review stored frames to find the correct timing for measurements",["Change transmit power","Change the transducer frequency","Increase PRF"],"Measurements such as end-diastole are taken by scrolling through the stored cine.","F"),
(I,"Line density refers to:","The number of scan lines per frame or per degree of sector",["The number of pixels per inch","The pulse length","The number of focal zones"],"Higher line density improves lateral detail but lowers frame rate.","F"),
(I,"Frame rate is calculated as:","1 ÷ time to make one frame",["PRF × depth","Lines × depth","Gain ÷ power"],"A frame taking 20 ms gives 50 frames per second.","F"),
(I,"Maximum frame rate is limited mainly by:","Speed of sound and imaging depth",["Monitor size","Output power","Gain"],"Each line must wait for echoes from the deepest point; sound speed in tissue is fixed.","F"),
(I,"Sector width affects frame rate because:","A wider sector needs more scan lines per frame",["A wider sector raises the PRF","A wider sector shortens the pulse","It changes the frequency"],"Narrowing the sector, at the same line density, raises frame rate.","F"),
# --- Artifacts ---
(X,"Artifacts arise because the machine assumes all of these EXCEPT:","Sound travels at different speeds in different tissues",["Sound travels in a straight line","Echoes come only from the main beam","Each reflection's strength comes from the reflector itself"],"The machine assumes 1540 m/s everywhere, straight paths, main-beam echoes, one bounce per echo and attenuation that is uniform. Breaking any of these causes artifacts.","C"),
(X,"Reverberation artifact appears as:","Multiple, equally spaced parallel echoes deep to two strong reflectors",["A copy of a structure across a strong reflector","A dark band behind a stone","A bright band behind a cyst"],"Sound bounces back and forth between two reflectors. Each extra round trip places another echo deeper.","F"),
(X,"Comet tail artifact is a type of reverberation in which:","The echoes are so close together they merge into a solid bright line",["Echoes are widely spaced","The image is reflected across the diaphragm","Shadowing occurs"],"It arises from small, highly reflective objects such as metal clips or calcium, with closely spaced reflecting surfaces.","F"),
(X,"Ring-down artifact is caused by:","Resonance of fluid trapped between small gas bubbles",["Two parallel strong reflectors far apart","Refraction at a lens","A speed error"],"The gas cluster vibrates and sends a continuous stream of sound back, seen as a bright streak.","C"),
(X,"Mirror image artifact appears:","Deeper than the true structure, on the far side of a strong reflector",["Shallower than the true structure","Beside the true structure at the same depth","Only in Doppler"],"Sound reflects off a strong specular reflector, hits the structure and returns along the same path, so the copy is placed farther away.","F"),
(X,"Which statement about the real structure and its mirror image is true?","The real structure and the strong reflector are both closer to the transducer than the artifact",["The artifact is closer to the transducer","They are at the same depth","The mirror image is brighter"],"The artifact lies on a straight line from the transducer through the real structure, beyond the reflector.","C"),
(X,"Acoustic shadowing is a:","Hypoechoic or anechoic area behind a strongly attenuating structure",["Bright area behind a weak attenuator","Copy of a structure","Series of parallel lines"],"Calcium, bone, prosthetic material and air all cast shadows.","F"),
(X,"Edge shadowing (refraction shadow) is seen:","At the edges of curved structures, from refraction and defocusing",["Behind all strong reflectors","Only behind bone","In front of curved structures"],"Sound bends at the edges of a curved interface, leaving thin dark lines below the edges.","F"),
(X,"The best way to see a structure hidden in an acoustic shadow is to:","Scan from a different window or angle",["Increase receiver gain","Add persistence","Raise the PRF"],"Shadowing is caused by real attenuation, so gain cannot recover echoes that never return. Image around the obstacle from another window.","F"),
(X,"Side lobe and grating lobe artifacts place echoes:","Along the main beam axis even though they came from off-axis reflectors",["Deeper than true depth","Shallower than true depth","At the correct location"],"The machine assumes every echo came from the main beam, so strong off-axis echoes are misplaced into the image line.","F"),
(X,"Harmonic imaging reduces lobe artifacts because:","Weak off-axis lobes create very few harmonics",["Harmonics travel faster","Harmonic beams are wider","Harmonics remove reverberation entirely"],"Harmonics form mostly where intensity is high, in the main beam.","F"),
(X,"Slice-thickness (section-thickness) artifact often appears as:","False echoes filling an anechoic structure, such as a cyst or chamber",["A dark band behind the structure","A copy below the diaphragm","Aliasing"],"Echoes from tissue just above or below the image plane are included because the beam has thickness.","F"),
(X,"Speed error (propagation speed error) occurs when sound travels slower than 1540 m/s. The reflector appears:","Deeper than it really is",["Shallower than it really is","At the correct depth","Duplicated beside itself"],"Echoes return later than expected, so the machine places them deeper. Faster media (e.g. bone) place them too shallow.","F"),
(X,"A 'step-off' or split in a straight boundary seen through a fatty layer is caused by:","Speed error",["Reverberation","Aliasing","Mirror image"],"Sound slows in fat, so echoes behind it are displayed slightly deeper than echoes beside it.","C"),
(X,"Range ambiguity artifact occurs when:","An echo from a deep structure returns after the next pulse has been sent, so it is placed too shallow",["The PRF is too low","Sound travels faster than assumed","Two reflectors are close together"],"Lowering the PRF (increasing depth) gives deep echoes time to return.","C"),
(X,"Multipath artifact occurs when:","The paths to and from a reflector differ, placing the echo too deep",["Sound refracts at a lens","Two reflectors are side by side","The PRF is too high"],"If the echo returns by a longer route, travel time increases and depth is overstated.","C"),
(X,"Refraction artifact can produce:","A duplicate structure beside the real one at the same depth",["A duplicate deeper than the real one","Shadowing only","Aliasing"],"The bent beam makes the machine place the reflector in the wrong lateral position.","F"),
(X,"Focal enhancement (focal banding) is:","A brighter horizontal band at the depth of the transmit focus",["A dark band behind a calcification","A copy of the focal zone","A Doppler artifact"],"Intensity is concentrated at the focus. Adjust TGC or the focus position to correct it.","F"),
(X,"Near-field clutter at the LV apex can mimic:","An apical thrombus",["A pericardial effusion","Aortic stenosis","An ASD"],"Reverberations from the chest wall fill the near field. Use harmonics, a higher frequency, zoom, other views or contrast to confirm a real mass.","F"),
(X,"A true LV apical thrombus differs from near-field clutter because a thrombus:","Moves with the wall, has defined borders and is seen in more than one view",["Stays fixed on the screen as the heart moves","Disappears with harmonics","Appears only at one depth on the screen regardless of view"],"Artifacts stay at a fixed distance from the transducer and don't move with the heart.","C"),
(X,"In echo, a mirror-image artifact of the heart or aorta may appear:","Deep to the pericardium-lung interface",["Superficial to the chest wall","Inside the left atrium","Only in M-mode"],"The pericardium and air-filled lung form a strong specular reflector, which can create a mirrored copy beyond it.","C"),
(X,"Reverberation artifacts from a mechanical prosthetic valve typically appear:","As bright streaks extending away from the transducer behind the valve",["As a copy beside the valve","Only in diastole","As aliasing"],"Metal is highly reflective, so reverberations and shadowing hide structures behind it. TEE helps image behind the prosthesis.","F"),
(X,"Enhancement artifact is caused by:","Low attenuation in a structure, so tissue behind it receives more sound",["High attenuation","Reverberation","Refraction"],"It is seen behind fluid-filled structures and can be partly corrected with TGC.","F"),
(X,"Which artifact is a 'real' sign that helps diagnosis?","Shadowing behind a gallstone or calcified valve",["Side lobe artifact","Range ambiguity","Electrical interference"],"Some artifacts, like shadowing (calcium) and enhancement (fluid), help characterize tissue.","F"),
(X,"Electrical interference usually appears as:","Regularly spaced lines or 'snow' across the image unrelated to anatomy",["A copy behind a reflector","Aliasing only","A dark band behind calcium"],"Nearby equipment can interfere. Check grounding and unplug other devices if possible.","F"),
(X,"Axial resolution artifact occurs when:","Two reflectors along the beam are closer than half the pulse length and appear as one",["Two side-by-side reflectors are narrower than the beam","Sound speed is wrong","PRF is too low"],"Fix by using a higher frequency, which shortens the pulse.","F"),
(X,"Lateral resolution artifact occurs when:","Two side-by-side reflectors are closer than the beam width and appear as one",["Two reflectors along the beam merge","Sound speed is wrong","Gain is too high"],"Fix by moving the focus to that depth or using a higher frequency.","F"),
(X,"Which two artifacts can place an echo SHALLOWER than its true depth?","Range ambiguity, and speed error in a fast medium",["Mirror image and multipath","Reverberation and comet tail","Slice thickness and side lobe"],"Most location artifacts place echoes too deep. Range ambiguity and speed error in a fast medium (such as bone or cartilage) place them too shallow.","C"),
(X,"To check whether a suspicious echo is an artifact, the best first step is to:","Image it from a different window or angle",["Increase gain","Freeze the image","Lower the frame rate"],"Real structures stay anatomically consistent in multiple views. Artifacts usually shift or disappear.","F"),
(X,"Reverberation artifacts are reduced by:","Changing the transducer angle or using harmonics",["Increasing gain","Increasing output power","Adding persistence"],"Avoid having the beam strike two strong parallel reflectors at 90°, and use tissue harmonics.","F"),
(X,"In TEE, a 'double aorta' appearance in the descending aorta is usually:","A mirror-image or reverberation artifact",["A true aortic dissection","A double aortic arch","Aliasing"],"The aortic wall and adjacent lung act as strong reflectors. Color Doppler and other views help exclude a dissection flap.","C"),
(X,"A linear echo in the ascending aorta that does not move independently, crosses anatomic boundaries and lies twice as deep as a strong reflector is most likely:","A reverberation artifact",["A dissection flap","A thrombus","A vegetation"],"True dissection flaps have independent motion, stay within the lumen and show different flow on each side with color Doppler.","C"),
(X,"Spectral Doppler mirror image is reduced by:","Lowering Doppler gain and improving the beam angle",["Raising Doppler gain","Raising the wall filter only","Lowering the PRF"],"The mirrored signal appears when gain is too high or the beam is nearly perpendicular.","F"),
(X,"Color Doppler 'flash' artifact is caused by:","Tissue or transducer motion, filling the image with color",["Aliasing of high velocities","A low wall filter only on slow flow","Electrical interference only"],"Motion of the heart, breathing or probe creates large Doppler shifts. Raise the wall filter or ask the patient to hold still.","F"),
(X,"Color 'ghosting' or blooming beyond the vessel walls is caused by:","Color gain set too high",["Color gain set too low","High wall filter","Low PRF"],"Reduce color gain until the color stays within the lumen.","F"),
(X,"Which artifact is caused by the assumption that sound travels in a straight line?","Refraction",["Reverberation","Speed error","Range ambiguity"],"Refraction bends the beam, but the machine plots echoes as if the beam went straight.","F"),
(X,"Which artifact is caused by the assumption that all echoes come from the main beam?","Side lobe artifact",["Mirror image","Shadowing","Speed error"],"Off-axis lobes return echoes that are plotted along the main axis.","F"),
(X,"Which artifact results from the assumption that sound travels at 1540 m/s?","Speed (propagation speed) error",["Shadowing","Side lobe","Slice thickness"],"Different tissue speeds displace echoes along the beam.","F"),
(X,"Which artifacts result from the assumption that echoes travel directly to a reflector and back?","Reverberation, mirror image and multipath",["Shadowing and enhancement","Side lobe and grating lobe","Slice thickness"],"Extra bounces lengthen the travel time, so echoes are placed too deep or duplicated.","C"),
(X,"Which artifacts result from the assumption that attenuation is uniform?","Shadowing and enhancement",["Reverberation and mirror image","Side lobe and grating lobe","Range ambiguity"],"TGC assumes average soft-tissue attenuation. Structures that attenuate more or less than that create dark or bright areas behind them.","F"),
(X,"Increasing the depth setting to remove range ambiguity works because it:","Lowers the PRF, giving more time for deep echoes to return",["Raises the PRF","Raises the frequency","Narrows the beam"],"With a longer listening period, echoes from deep structures arrive before the next pulse.","C"),
(X,"Which is NOT an artifact?","Spontaneous echo contrast ('smoke') in a dilated left atrium with slow flow",["Reverberation","Side lobe","Mirror image"],"Smoke is a real finding: red cell aggregates in slow-moving blood, a risk factor for thrombus.","C"),
(X,"Beam-width artifact in the left atrium can create:","A false mass from echoes of nearby structures outside the scan plane",["Aliasing in the LA","Real thrombus","Mitral stenosis"],"Thick beams include off-plane echoes from structures such as the descending aorta or atrial wall, mimicking a mass.","C"),
(X,"Using a higher frequency to reduce near-field clutter works because:","It improves near-field resolution and reduces reverberation from the chest wall",["It increases penetration","It lowers the PRF","It removes shadowing"],"Near-field structures like the apex are close to the transducer, so higher frequency improves detail there.","C"),
(X,"Shadowing behind a mechanical mitral prosthesis on transthoracic echo can hide:","Mitral regurgitation jets in the left atrium",["The LV apex","The aortic arch","The RV free wall"],"From the apex, the prosthesis lies in front of the LA, so its shadow can mask MR. TEE views the LA from behind and avoids this.","C"),
(X,"Grating lobe artifacts are most common with:","Array transducers, especially when steering at wide angles",["Single-element transducers","CW pencil probes","Annular arrays with no steering"],"Grating lobes come from the regular spacing of array elements; subdicing and apodization reduce them.","F"),
(X,"An anechoic chamber appears to contain weak echoes that disappear when the transducer is tilted slightly. This is most likely:","Slice-thickness artifact",["A thrombus","A vegetation","Spontaneous contrast"],"True masses persist with small changes in angle and in other views.","C"),
(X,"A structure appears twice, side by side at the same depth, when imaging through the rectus muscle or near the pericardial fat. This is:","Refraction (ghost) artifact",["Mirror image","Reverberation","Comet tail"],"Refraction at a lens-shaped tissue boundary bends the beam, producing a side-by-side duplicate.","C"),
(X,"Increasing the number of focal zones to correct focal banding will:","Even out brightness with depth but lower the frame rate",["Raise the frame rate","Change the wavelength","Remove shadowing"],"Multiple foci spread the intensity peak over several depths.","C"),
]
